Clean up python (make clean should remove python stuff, as should clean_coverage).

Investigate IDACalcIC taking too many steps at initial time.

Investigate occasional wiggles in outputs.

Left over from the TransportSystem interface work. Everything else in that plan
landed; these are the pieces that were deliberately not done, with the reason.

  - Per-hook vectorisation in the Python trampoline. `override_for("SigmaFn_v")`
    and the other `_v` names are looked up but never inserted into
    method_overrides, so they can only throw: implementing `ComputePhysics` and
    `ComputePhysicsDerivatives` together remains the only vectorised route. A
    case that wants to vectorise one hook and leave the rest pointwise cannot.
    Registering the `_v` names in initializeOverrides is most of the fix; the
    rest is dropping the all-or-nothing classification.

  - The autodiff Source still receives the *stored* sigma, not sigma_hat.
    State::sigmaHat exists and is correct, but AutodiffTransportSystem::Sources
    builds its RealVector from the stored (negated) value, so a case that starts
    using its sigma argument gets the opposite sign from the one SigmaFn
    returned. Flipping it also needs a compensating negation in
    dSources_dsigma, because autodiff would then differentiate with respect to
    sigma_hat while the solver wants d/d(stored sigma).

    The reason this is not already done: *nothing in the tree reads the incoming
    sigma at all*. MirrorPlasma and AdjointPlasma thread it into
    Sn/Spi/Spe/Somega without using it, and every dSources_dsigma is identically
    zero. So the change is unobservable today and no test would catch getting it
    wrong -- which is exactly why it should not be made blind. Write a physics
    case with a genuinely sigma-dependent source first, check it against a
    closed form, then flip.

  - GlobalState still crosses to Python as a dict of (nPoints, nVars) arrays
    keyed "Variable"/"Derivative"/"Flux"/"Aux"/"Scalars". State became a named
    view; GlobalState did not, because a dict of whole-batch arrays is what the
    vectorised and JAX paths actually want. Worth revisiting only if the key
    strings start causing the same trouble the pointwise ones did.

  - The out-of-tree paths are verified by hand, not by CI. Both were exercised
    end to end -- a one-file C++ case compiled against the installed headers,
    dlopened via PhysicsPlugins and run; and `pip install .` into a fresh venv
    with a Python case run from the user's own directory through the `manta`
    command -- but neither is an automated test. The C++ one needs `make
    install` to a temp prefix and a compiler invocation; the Python one needs a
    venv. Both are slow enough to want their own CI job rather than a place in
    `make test`.

  - MirrorPlasma's useConstantVoltage scalars are unexercised. The port from
    nested adaptive quadrature to the framework's node quadrature rests on the
    algebra alone: nothing in any suite turns that mode on. checkScalarDerivative
    in ScalarJacobianTests.cpp would check its Jacobian against a finite
    difference of its own ScalarG for free, given a fixture that configures it.

  - Physics cases now name their own variables, and netCDF groups take those
    names. Two consequences for anything written earlier: an analysis script
    that reads MirrorPlasma output by "Var0".."Var3" needs the channel names
    instead (Density, IonEnergy, ElectronEnergy, AngularMomentum), and a
    netCDF initial-condition file saved before the rename has groups the case no
    longer looks for -- LoadDataToSpline reads getVariableName(i) now and will
    say so rather than silently mis-load. Cases whose width comes from the
    config (MatrixDiffusion, MatrixDiffusionTest, LinearDiffSourceTest) keep the
    numbered placeholder names, which is why testic.nc is still valid.


Superconvergence follow-ons (see Tests/README.md for what is already verified):

  - Paper II's HDG(ABC) / HDG+ family: W_h = P_{k+1} with tau ~ 1/h makes u_h
    itself superconverge, with no postprocessing, and reaches k+2 at k = 0 too.
    Needs mixed polynomial degrees across fields, which the single-k assumption in
    DGSoln::Map, every block in Matrices.cpp, AdjointVectors.cpp, the pybind11
    casters and the restart format all rest on. tau ~ 1/h also changes every
    existing run.

  - An order study for a general nonlinear flux sigma_hat(u, q). Paper I covers
    -Laplacian u + F(u) only, and names F(grad u, u) as open, so the k+2 rate for
    MaNTA's flux is currently unasserted. SolveJacTests covers the Jacobian for
    such a flux; only the rate is missing.

  - Order studies with nAux > 0 and nScalars > 0 under Superconvergent = true.
    The Jacobian handles both; nothing measures the rate.

  - Spatial adjoint parameters with Superconvergent = true currently throw. They
    index the parameter vector by node, so the star node set would redefine how
    many parameters there are.


Diagnose why a second integration on the same SystemSolver fails. IDASolve
returns IDA_ERR_FAIL (-3) on the first step of the second run, whether via a
second runSolver() or a second initialize()/integrate() pair, and whether or not
the first run completed. Pre-existing -- b7d8031 fails identically -- and hidden
because PyRunner::configure builds a fresh SystemSolver every time. Candidates:
state a completed run leaves in RF_cellwise / updateBoundaryConditions, and the
nc_output time index. Tests/README.md has the detail.

Fix the id vector: IDA is told the whole system is algebraic. Solver.cpp's
initialize() means to mark u as differential and writes

    isDifferential.u(v).getCoeff(i).second.Constant(k + 1, 1.0);

but Eigen's Constant is a *static factory* -- the call is legal through an
instance, returns a fresh expression and discards it, so the line is a no-op and
`id` keeps the zeros zeroCoeffs() left. Verified directly: N_VL1Norm(id) is 0
after initialize(). Wants .setConstant(1.0). Nothing warns, because Constant is
not [[nodiscard]] and the statement has no unused *variable* for -Wall to notice.

Consequences are not obvious and want measuring before the one-word fix lands:
IDASetId feeds IDA_YA_YDP_INIT, which uses id to decide which components of y to
solve for and which components of y' to compute, so at present IDACalcIC is doing
something other than intended for every run in the tree -- and every checked-in
.ref.nc was generated that way. Expect the regression references to move, and
budget for deciding whether the new answers are the right ones rather than
regenerating on sight. The dG/dt gate below is the first thing to re-examine
afterwards, since what it can see depends on this.

dG/dt at t0 covers only the differential part of the state. The gate assembles a
full chain rule over u, q, sigma and phi -- SystemSolver::dGdt, verified against a
finite difference of a real objective functional in AdjointProblemTests.cpp -- but
at t0 the q, sigma and phi blocks of dydt are identically zero, so those terms
contribute nothing and an objective is differentiated through its u dependence
alone. Two causes: those fields are algebraic, and IDA_YA_YDP_INIT computes
algebraic values and differential derivatives, so there is no y' for them to
fetch; and the id bug above means IDA's idea of which is which is wrong anyway.
Pinned by at_t0_only_the_differential_part_of_dydt_exists in
SolverLifecycleTests.cpp, which fails if either changes.

Closing it means getting the algebraic time derivatives, which are determined by
differentiating the constraints -- q' = d(u')/dx, sigma' from differentiating
SigmaFn, phi' from differentiating AuxG -- i.e. one linear solve against the same
Jacobian the forward solve already builds. The cheaper alternative is to move the
gate after IDA's first step, where dYdt is fully populated, trading one step for
completeness. Neither is done.

Which state should t0 output report? initialiseNetCDF writes its t0 timeslice from
Y before IDAGetConsistentIC has been called, so the "initial condition" in the
netCDF is the pre-CalcIC one, differing from the corrected state by around 1e-8
relative on the regression cases. The gate fetches the corrected state only when
it is armed, specifically so that ungated output stays bit-for-bit unchanged; if
the answer is that t0 should report the corrected state, that condition can go.

IDACalcIC's return value is discarded. Solver.cpp reads

    retval = IDACalcIC(...);
    retval = 0;

so the "IDACalcIC could not complete" throw below it can never fire and a failed
IDACalcIC carries on silently. Matters for the dG/dt gate in particular, which
would then be testing a meaningless initial condition.

